###### Abstract

We report the discovery of PSR J1101-6101, a 62.8 ms pulsar in IGR J11014-6103, a hard X-ray source with a jet and a cometary tail that strongly suggests it is moving away from the center of the supernova remnant (SNR) MSH 11-6A at v>1000 km s{}^{-1}. Two _XMM-Newton_ observations were obtained with the EPIC pn in small window mode, resulting in the measurement of its spin-down luminosity \\dot{E}=1.36\\times 10^{36} erg s{}^{-1}, characteristic age \\tau_{c}=116 kyr, and surface magnetic field strength B_{s}=7.4\\times 10^{11} G. In comparison to \\tau_{c}, the 10-30 kyr age estimated for MSH 11-6A suggests that the pulsar was born in the SNR with initial period in the range 54\\leq P_{0}\\leq 60 ms. PSR J1101-6101 is the least energetic of the 15 rotation-powered pulsars detected by _INTEGRAL_, and has a high efficiency of hard X-ray radiation and jet power. We examine the shape of the cometary nebula in a _Chandra_ image, which is roughly consistent with a bow shock at the velocity inferred from the SNR age and the pulsar's \\dot{E}. However, its structure differs in detail from the classic bow shock, and we explore possible reasons for this.

